---

If you're on the Free plan and your storage keeps creeping toward the limit, old deployments are a likely reason.

Every time you push to Git or redeploy, your function or site gets a new deployment, and the old one sticks around. Only one of them, the active deployment, serves your traffic. The rest are inactive, but each one is still a full copy of your code and build, and it takes up storage. On the Free plan, that's the same 2GB your files and databases use. A few months of pushes can fill most of it.

From **November 5, 2026**, you won't have to clean them up by hand. By default, inactive deployments on Appwrite Cloud are cleaned up once they're more than **30 days** old, and the space goes back to your files and databases. If you want more history, you can keep them for longer, up to a maximum that depends on your plan. Your active deployment always stays.

# More room on the Free plan

Old deployments are why many Free plan users run out of storage. Say each deployment of your site is 40MB and you deploy twice a week. After six months, that's about 50 deployments and your whole 2GB. With the cleanup, only the deployments you created in the last 30 days stay: about 9 of them, or roughly 350MB.

On the Free and Education plans, each function or site also keeps only its **100** newest inactive deployments, so a project that deploys many times a day can't pile up hundreds of them within those 30 days.

To see how much space your deployments use today, open your project's **Overview** page, select **Storage**, and choose **Functions** or **Sites** in the list next to the chart.

# What always stays

Two kinds of deployments are never cleaned up, however old they are:

- The **active deployment** of each function and site, the one serving your traffic.
- Any deployment a **custom domain** points to, such as a domain pinned to a Git branch.

When an inactive deployment is cleaned up, its preview URL stops working too.

# Keep deployments for longer

Today, every function and site is set to **Keep deployments forever** unless you've changed it. On November 5, those switch to 30 days, and the first cleanup removes every inactive deployment that's already older than that. If you want more history for rollbacks, choose a longer retention period before then.

Open your function or site in the Console and go to **Settings** > **Build** > **Retention**. Turn off **Keep deployments forever**, choose a **Retention period** up to your plan's maximum, and click **Update**.

| Plan | Default | Maximum | Deployments kept |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Free | 30 days | 90 days | Newest 100 |
| Education | 30 days | 90 days | Newest 100 |
| Start | 30 days | 3 years | No limit |
| Pro | 30 days | 3 years | No limit |

Enterprise plans start with the same options as Pro. For a longer or custom policy, [talk to our sales team](/sales).

If you've already picked a longer period than your plan allows, it drops to your plan's maximum: a Free function set to 1 year keeps 90 days.

A deployment's age counts from the day you created it, not the day it stopped being active. Say your site has run the same deployment for six months. When you push a new one, the old one is already more than 30 days old, so it's cleaned up within about an hour. If you might need it again, download its source code first so you can redeploy it later.

To keep a copy of a deployment's files, download it before it's cleaned up. On the **Deployments** tab, open the deployment's menu and choose **Download** > **Source code** or **Build output**.

# Self-hosted Appwrite

Nothing changes on self-hosted Appwrite. Deployments are kept forever by default, and you can set any retention period you like.

